content.push(ScriptBlock("Hey [first name], no rush on this.\n\nThe reason I asked is that Bethany Anne (the founder I refer to) has been seeing a pattern in practices around the $500K to $2M mark. Almost none of them have a lead problem. The revenue is leaking somewhere between the lead coming in and the client coming back, and most owners cannot see it from inside the business.\n\nIf I am off base, just say so and I will get out of your inbox. If it is at all on-base, I am happy to share what she usually finds.\n\n[Your name]"));

content.push(H2("Email 3: The walk-away (7 days after Email 2, only if no reply)"));
content.push(P("Subject: last one from me"));
content.push(Spacer());
content.push(ScriptBlock("Hey [first name],\n\nNot going to keep crowding your inbox. If the timing is wrong, the timing is wrong.\n\nIf you ever want to know where money is quietly leaking in a practice your size, Bethany Anne runs a 45-minute Revenue Leak Audit for $29. She walks you through exactly where the gaps usually are and you keep the KPI Calculator afterward. Link is here if you ever want it: [LINK].\n\nEither way, wish you a good rest of the quarter.\n\n[Your name]"));
content.push(Spacer());
content.push(Callout("Why the walk-away works",
  "The walk-away email outperforms the bump email almost every time. It releases pressure, gives them permission to ignore you, and leaves the door open. Owners who were on the fence often book after this exact email."));

content.push(H2("Email reply scripts (when they actually respond)"));
content.push(H3("If they reply with a real pain point"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("Yeah, that is the one Bethany Anne sees most often in practices your size. [Mirror what they said in one sentence]. The reason it persists is usually [name the underlying pattern: the front desk is undertrained for sales / there is no real package architecture / consults are converting but retention drops off after treatment two].\n\nIf you want, I can connect you with Bethany Anne for her Revenue Leak Audit. It is a paid 45-minute consultation, $29, and she walks you through exactly where the gaps are in your specific market plus you keep her KPI Calculator. Want me to send the link?"));

content.push(H3("If they reply with skepticism or pushback"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("Totally fair. I would push back too if a stranger emailed me out of nowhere.\n\nThe quick version: Bethany Anne has been in medical aesthetics for over a decade and has actually built and run practices, not just consulted on them. The Revenue Leak Audit is $29 because she only wants to talk to owners who are serious enough to put a little skin in. If after 45 minutes you think it is generic or unhelpful, that is a $29 lesson. If it is the real thing, you save six figures.\n\nNo pressure either way. Just want to make sure you have it if it is useful."));

content.push(H3("If they say \"send me info\""));
content.push(ScriptBlock("I could send a deck, but honestly the deck will not tell you what is actually happening in your specific practice. That is the whole point of the audit. It is built around your numbers, your market, your team, not a generic case study.\n\nIf you want the short version, the site is theluxoracollective.co. But the call is where you actually find out if it is a fit. $29, 45 minutes, and you keep the KPI Calculator either way. Want the link?"));

content.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));

// =========================================================================
// CHANNEL: PHONE
// =========================================================================
content.push(H1("Channel two: Phone"));
content.push(P("Phone is the highest-conversion channel in the entire stack and the channel most setters avoid. If an owner picks up, you have 15 seconds to earn the next 60. If you earn the next 60, you have a real conversation."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H2("Phone principles"));
content.push(Bullet("Call between 10am and 11:30am local, or between 2pm and 3:30pm local. Avoid Mondays and Fridays."));
content.push(Bullet("Open with their name, your name, and the reason you are calling. In that order."));
content.push(Bullet("Pattern-interrupt the first 10 seconds. They are expecting a pitch. Give them a question."));
content.push(Bullet("Never start with \"how are you today.\" That phrase is the verbal flag for cold sales."));
content.push(Bullet("Match their pace. If they sound rushed, you sound rushed. If they slow down, you slow down."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H2("Phone script: When the owner picks up"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("Hey [first name], this is [your name]. I know I am calling out of the blue so I will be quick. I refer med spa owners to a team called The Luxora Collective and I was actually looking at [practice name] this morning. Is now a bad time for me to ask one question, or should I try you back?"));
content.push(P("That last line is the lock. \"Is now a bad time\" gives them an easy out, which paradoxically makes them more likely to say no, it is fine, keep going. If they say it is a bad time, you ask when to call back and you call back."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H3("If they give you the green light"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("Appreciate it. So the one question is this. From the outside, it looks like you have built a real practice. But Bethany Anne, the founder I refer to, sees a pattern in practices your size where the lead flow is actually fine but everything after the lead, the booking, the consult, the retention, is where money is quietly leaking. Does any of that sound familiar in your business, or are you in a different spot?"));

content.push(H3("If they say yes to one of those friction points"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("Yeah, that is the one I hear most. [Mirror what they said]. The reason Bethany Anne is good at this is she has actually run med spas, not just consulted on them. She runs a paid 45-minute call she calls a Revenue Leak Audit, $29, and she walks owners through exactly where the gaps usually are plus you keep her KPI Calculator after. Would it be useful for me to text you the link so you can grab a time that works?"));

content.push(H3("If they push back on the price"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("Totally get it, most people charge nothing for these. That is exactly why she charges $29. It filters out tire-kickers so the calls she does take are higher quality. And for $29 you get the diagnostic plus the calculator, so even if you never work with her again, it pays for itself. Want me to text you the link?"));

content.push(H3("If they say \"send me an email\""));
content.push(ScriptBlock("For sure, I will send you something. But quick question. If I send you something and you read it, what would actually move you to book a call? Because honestly, if the answer is nothing, I would rather not waste either of our time. I want to send something you will actually use."));
content.push(P("That question is a soft mirror. It puts the decision back in their court. Most owners either say \"actually you can just text me the link\" or they go quiet and you send a short follow-up email."));

content.push(H2("Voicemail script (when they do not pick up)"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("Hey [first name], [your name] here. Reaching out about [practice name]. I refer owners to a team called The Luxora Collective and yours came up when they were mapping practices in your area. Not a pitch, just had a question for you. I will follow up with a text and an email so you have options. Talk soon."));
content.push(P("Keep voicemails under 20 seconds. The point is not to sell, it is to make your name familiar so the follow-up text and email land warmer."));

content.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));

// =========================================================================
// CHANNEL: TEXT
// =========================================================================
content.push(H1("Channel three: Text message"));
content.push(P("Text is the most intimate channel you have. It also has the highest open rate of anything in this playbook. Use it after a phone call, a voicemail, or when the owner has given you their number through a form or a referral. Do not cold-text someone whose number you found on a database."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H2("Text principles"));
content.push(Bullet("Two to four sentences max."));
content.push(Bullet("Lowercase. Casual. No corporate punctuation."));
content.push(Bullet("Always say who you are in the first text. Always."));
content.push(Bullet("Never send the booking link in the first text."));
content.push(Bullet("Mirror their texting style. If they use periods, you use periods. If they do not, you do not."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H2("Text 1: Post-voicemail follow-up"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("hey [first name], this is [your name], just left you a voicemail. no rush. quick question about [practice name] when you have a sec. text is fine if easier than a call."));

content.push(H2("Text 2: If they respond \"what is up\" or \"who is this\""));
content.push(ScriptBlock("good to hear back. so the quick one, i refer owners to bethany anne at the luxora collective and she has been mapping practices in your area. yours came up. just wanted to ask, from the outside it looks like you have the front of the business handled, but where does it actually break down for you most often: bookings, consult conversion, or retention?"));

content.push(H2("Text 3: If they engage with a real answer"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("yeah that is the one bethany anne sees most in practices your size. she runs a 45-minute revenue leak audit, $29, walks you through exactly where the gaps are plus you keep her kpi calculator. want me to send the link?"));

content.push(H2("Text 4: If they say yes, send the link"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("here you go: [LINK]. grab whatever time works. she runs most of these herself right now so you are not getting handed off. let me know how it goes."));

content.push(H2("Text 5: The cold restart (30 to 45 days after no reply)"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("hey [first name], circling back one time. no agenda. bethany anne just put together a new market intelligence briefing on [their city] specifically for med spas in the $500k to $2m range. want me to send it over? free, no call required."));
content.push(P("This text works because it changes the offer. You are not asking for time, you are giving them something. Even if they do not respond, you have warmed the relationship for the next round."));

content.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));

// =========================================================================
// CHANNEL: INSTAGRAM DM
// =========================================================================
content.push(H1("Channel four: Instagram DM"));
content.push(P("Instagram is where most med spa owners live emotionally. It is also where the line between authentic and creepy is thinnest. The rule is simple. You earn the right to DM by engaging with their content first, genuinely, for at least three to seven days before you ever send a message."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H2("Instagram principles"));
content.push(Bullet("Warm the relationship before the DM. Like real posts. Leave one thoughtful comment, not five emojis."));
content.push(Bullet("Never DM cold from a fresh profile. Your profile should look like a real person."));
content.push(Bullet("Reference something specific from their content in the first message. Not their face, not their body, not their personal life. Their business."));
content.push(Bullet("Keep DMs the length of a text. Two to four sentences max."));
content.push(Bullet("Voice notes work, but only after they have replied to two text DMs. Never lead with a voice note."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H2("The 3-day warmup before you DM"));
content.push(Bullet("Day 1: Follow them. Like their three most recent business posts (not their selfies)."));
content.push(Bullet("Day 2: Leave one specific comment on a business post. Reference the content, not the person. Example: \"Curious what your average package size landed at after you restructured the membership tiers.\""));
content.push(Bullet("Day 3: Engage with one of their stories if they post one. Reply to the story with a quick reaction or a real question."));
content.push(Bullet("Day 4 to 7: Send the opening DM."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H2("Instagram DM 1: The opener"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("hey [first name], been following along for a bit, the way you broke down your team structure in that reel last week was sharp. most owners i see do not actually share that operational view. quick question, behind the scenes at [practice name] right now, what is the part of the business that is actually slowing you down? bookings, consult conversion, retention?"));

content.push(H2("Instagram DM 2: If they reply with a real answer"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("yeah that is the one bethany anne sees the most in practices your size. she is the founder of the luxora collective, ten plus years in medical aesthetics, has actually built and run med spas herself. she runs a 45-minute revenue leak audit, $29, and walks you through exactly where the gaps are in your specific market. want me to send the link or is this the wrong moment?"));

content.push(H2("Instagram DM 3: If they reply with curiosity but not commitment"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("totally get it. the short version: bethany anne does not do the agency thing. inside-out methodology, finds the leaks first before recommending any new spend. the $29 call is the diagnostic. you keep the kpi calculator either way. zero pressure, just an open door if you want it. here is the link if you ever want it: [LINK]"));

content.push(H2("Instagram DM 4: The story reply opener (alternate path)"));
content.push(P("If they post a story about being slammed, frustrated with staff, or hitting a wall, you can reply to the story directly. This is a soft and effective opener."));
content.push(Spacer());
content.push(ScriptBlock("that story hit. that exhausted-by-your-own-success feeling is the most common thing i hear from owners your size and it is almost never a marketing problem, it is a systems one. happy to share what bethany anne usually sees in practices like yours if you ever want to know."));

content.push(H2("Instagram comment scripts (for warming, before DMing)"));
content.push(P("Use these as templates. Customize to the actual post. Never copy-paste the same comment on multiple profiles."));
content.push(Bullet("On a team or staff post: \"Curious how you handled the hiring filter on this. Most practices at this stage struggle to keep a closer on the front desk.\""));
content.push(Bullet("On a service launch post: \"What was the conversion rate when you rolled this out to your existing client base versus new bookings?\""));
content.push(Bullet("On a before-and-after post: \"This is the kind of result that should be the anchor of your retention conversation. Are you packaging this as a series or one-off right now?\""));
content.push(Bullet("On a transformation reel: \"Real question, are you finding the clients who book this are also the ones who upgrade to memberships, or is it a different segment?\""));
content.push(P("These comments do two things at once. They demonstrate you understand the business at a real level. They give the owner a reason to look at your profile and consider replying."));

content.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));

// =========================================================================
// CHANNEL: FACEBOOK GROUPS
// =========================================================================
content.push(H1("Channel five: Facebook med spa groups"));
content.push(P("Facebook groups are where med spa owners go to ask the questions they cannot ask in public. They are also where every consultant, coach, and vendor lurks waiting to pitch. The owners in these groups have built up a strong sniff test. You will only win in these spaces if you give before you take, and if you never pitch in a thread."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H2("Facebook group principles"));
content.push(Bullet("Never pitch in the group. Never. Not even subtly."));
content.push(Bullet("Comment on questions where you actually have something useful to say. Long-form, no links, no calls to action."));
content.push(Bullet("Build a posting cadence. Two genuinely helpful comments per week, minimum."));
content.push(Bullet("DM owners only after they have responded to one of your group comments. Reference the comment when you DM."));
content.push(Bullet("Never tag Bethany Anne or Luxora in a thread unless someone has already brought the company up by name."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H2("Groups to be active in"));
content.push(Bullet("Medical Aesthetic Practice Owners (private, vet required)."));
content.push(Bullet("MedSpa Industry Insiders."));
content.push(Bullet("Aesthetic Practice Success."));
content.push(Bullet("Injectors and Aesthetic Professionals."));
content.push(Bullet("Local and regional med spa owner groups in your geography."));
content.push(P("If you are not already in these groups, request access using your real name and a real reason. Mention you refer owners to a growth team and you are there to learn from owners directly."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H2("Facebook group post engagements (templates)"));

content.push(H3("On a post about staff turnover"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("staff turnover at this stage is almost always a symptom, not the root. when i talk to owners in your range, the pattern is usually one of three things: the compensation structure does not reward retention behavior, the front desk role is undertrained and gets blamed for stuff that is actually an ops problem, or the owner is still the only closer in the building so the team does not feel ownership of conversion. happy to share what i usually see if you want to dm me, no agenda."));

content.push(H3("On a post about slow months or seasonality"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("seasonality is real but in my experience it covers up a bigger pattern. most practices in the $500k to $2m range have a retention leak that only becomes visible during the slow months because there is no buffer of returning clients to smooth out the dip. the fix is rarely more leads, it is package architecture and a real membership tier that creates predictable revenue. happy to dig into specifics if you want to dm."));

content.push(H3("On a post about whether to hire a marketing agency"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("the question i would ask first is whether your current consult conversion and retention numbers can actually handle more lead flow. most practices that hire an agency are paying for a bigger top of funnel when the real leak is below that line. if you are converting 30 percent of consults and most clients do not come back after treatment two, more leads just amplifies the same leak. just my two cents from watching this play out over and over."));

content.push(H3("On a post asking for vendor recommendations"));
content.push(P("In general, do not name Luxora in the thread unless someone else does first. If someone else mentions Luxora or Bethany Anne, you can validate."));
content.push(Spacer());
content.push(ScriptBlock("can vouch for bethany anne and the luxora team. i refer owners to her and the model is different than most. she finds the leaks inside the practice first before recommending any new spend, and most owners recover six figures from the leak audit alone. happy to dm if you want more context."));

content.push(H3("On a post asking about a specific service or revenue stream"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("the question is not whether to add the service, it is whether your existing client base will buy it without you having to acquire new traffic. if you have a strong membership and package architecture, a new service launches into demand you already created. if you are starting from cold every time you launch something, you are paying to acquire the same client over and over. that is the real cost most owners do not price in."));

content.push(H2("Group post template (when you actually post your own content)"));
content.push(P("Post your own content sparingly. Once every two to three weeks at most. When you do, the post should be educational, no links, no asks, and should generate comments that you can engage with."));
content.push(Spacer());
content.push(ScriptBlock("a pattern i keep seeing in med spas in the $500k to $2m range:\n\nthe owner thinks they have a lead problem. they spend more on ads. the new leads come in. the front desk is still undertrained for sales handoff. consults still convert at the same rate. retention still drops off after treatment two.\n\nthe ad spend doubled. the revenue did not.\n\nthe leak is almost never at the top of the funnel. it is between lead and booking, booking and consult, consult and second visit.\n\ncurious for the owners here, when you actually trace your last 10 leads through every touchpoint, where is the biggest drop-off?"));
content.push(P("That post is doing three things. It demonstrates you understand the business. It surfaces a pattern owners recognize. It ends with a question that invites comments, which is what gives you the right to DM owners after they engage."));

content.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));

// =========================================================================
// CHANNEL: LINKEDIN
// =========================================================================
content.push(H1("Channel six: LinkedIn"));
content.push(P("LinkedIn is the channel where med spa owners are at their most professional and least guarded. They are not getting pitched as hard there as they are on Instagram, which means a thoughtful message stands out. The catch is that LinkedIn rewards substance. A thin message dies."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H2("LinkedIn principles"));
content.push(Bullet("Connect first with a personal note. Never use the default \"I'd like to add you to my network\" connection."));
content.push(Bullet("Wait three to five days after they accept before sending the first real message."));
content.push(Bullet("First message can be longer than DM or text. Five to seven sentences is fine."));
content.push(Bullet("Reference their LinkedIn content specifically. If they do not post on LinkedIn, reference their About section or recent role change."));
content.push(Bullet("Never InMail cold. Connection request first."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H2("LinkedIn connection request"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("hi [first name], been following [practice name] and the way you have positioned the business in [city] stands out. i refer med spa owners to a growth team called the luxora collective and they have been mapping practices in your area. would love to connect."));
content.push(P("Keep it under 300 characters. LinkedIn cuts off at that point. Do not pitch in the connection request."));

content.push(H2("LinkedIn message 1: The opener (3 to 5 days after they accept)"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("Hi [first name], appreciate the connect.\n\nQuick context on why I reached out. I refer independent med spa owners to a team called The Luxora Collective and they have been mapping practices in your area as part of a market intelligence project. [Practice name] kept coming up as one that is clearly built something real.\n\nBethany Anne (the founder I refer to) has been seeing a pattern in practices in the $500K to $2M range. Almost none of them have a lead problem. The revenue is leaking somewhere between lead and consult or consult and long-term retention, and most owners cannot see it from inside the business.\n\nCurious where the friction actually lives in your business right now. Is it more on the front end with bookings and consults, or on the back end with retention and repeat revenue?"));

content.push(H2("LinkedIn message 2: If they engage"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("That tracks with the pattern Bethany Anne sees in practices your size. [Mirror what they said in one sentence].\n\nIf you are open to it, she runs a 45-minute Revenue Leak Audit. It is a paid consultation, $29, and she walks you through exactly where the gaps are in your specific market plus you keep her KPI Calculator. The $29 is the qualifier, not a barrier. She only takes calls with owners serious enough to put a little skin in.\n\nHappy to send the link if it is useful."));

content.push(H2("LinkedIn message 3: The walk-away (if no reply after two messages)"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("Hi [first name], no pressure on this.\n\nNot going to keep crowding your messages. If the timing is wrong, the timing is wrong. If you ever want to take a look at where revenue might be leaking in a practice your size, the link is here: [LINK]. $29, 45 minutes, you keep the KPI Calculator either way.\n\nWishing you a strong rest of the quarter."));

content.push(H2("LinkedIn post engagements"));
content.push(P("If the owner posts on LinkedIn, comment on their posts before you DM. Comments on LinkedIn should be longer and more substantive than Instagram. Two to three sentences minimum."));
content.push(Spacer());
content.push(H3("On a post about business growth or a milestone"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("Congrats on this. The thing most owners do not say out loud at this stage is how much harder the next jump is than the last one. The systems that got you to where you are now are usually the same ones that bottleneck the next stage. Curious what you are prioritizing as you scale into the next chapter."));

content.push(H3("On a post about hiring or team building"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("This is the part of the business most consultants do not touch. Hiring at this stage is not a recruiting problem, it is a role architecture problem. If the role itself is not designed to scale, the hire never will either. Always interesting to see how owners are solving for this."));

content.push(H3("On a post about industry trends"));
content.push(ScriptBlock("Agree on most of this. The piece I would push back on gently is [specific point]. From what I am seeing across independent practices in the $500K to $2M range, the pattern is actually [your observation]. Curious if you are seeing the same."));

content.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));

// =========================================================================
// SECTION: WHO THEY ARE TALKING TO
// =========================================================================
content.push(H1("If they ask: who is this?"));
content.push(P("Use this language verbatim. Do not improvise on credentials. The owners we want will read between the lines, so make sure the right signals are there."));
content.push(Spacer());
content.push(ScriptBlock("The team is The Luxora Collective. The website is theluxoracollective.co.\n\nIt is led by Bethany Anne, Co-Founder and CEO. She has spent over a decade in medical aesthetics, both inside multi-location practices on the operations side and on the growth side. She has built and managed med spas herself, which is why Luxora does not sound like a typical agency.\n\nShe has a direct hand in every client's growth plan from start to finish, so even though the team handles a lot of the execution, the strategy and the work itself runs through her. You are not getting handed off to a junior account manager.\n\nHer business partner Eric is the CFO and runs the financial side, and they have a full team behind them handling tech, paid traffic, content, and client delivery. It is not a one-person shop, but Bethany Anne stays close to the work."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H1("If they ask: what do they actually do?"));
content.push(P("Lead with the inside-out methodology. Luxora handles the full revenue engine, including paid traffic, but starts by fixing what is leaking before scaling spend."));
content.push(Spacer());
content.push(ScriptBlock("The easiest way to describe it is they work inside-out. Most teams in this space start with ads. Luxora starts with the business itself.\n\nStep one is a Revenue Leak Audit. They find the money already being lost inside the practice: undertrained front desk staff, unconverted consults, stagnant clients who never come back, missing memberships, weak package architecture. Most practices recover six figures here before scaling any new spend.\n\nStep two is a Market Blueprint specific to that practice and that city, so the offer, pricing, and positioning are built on actual data instead of guesses.\n\nStep three is installing the full Growth Engine: staff sales training, memberships, package and upsell architecture, AI follow-up, CRM, reputation engine, partnership frameworks, LTV systems, and several optional add-on playbooks (longevity, peptides, and wellness through clinical partners) for owners who want a new revenue stream. Her team also runs Meta ads when it makes sense, but only after the inside is fixed so the spend actually converts.\n\nIt is a done-with-you model. They do not take the keys, they teach the owner and team how to run it. Most clients work with them for a year or longer."));

content.push(H2("The one-liner version"));
content.push(P("If they want it in a single sentence."));
content.push(Spacer());
content.push(ScriptBlock("Luxora finds where med spas are leaking revenue, audits whether their current ad spend is actually built for their market, and installs the systems so bookings, conversions, and client retention all run predictably without the owner being in every transaction."));

content.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));

// =========================================================================
// OBJECTION HANDLING
// =========================================================================
content.push(H1("Objection handling"));
content.push(P("Every objection follows the same three-beat structure. Acknowledge. Reframe. Redirect to the conversation. Never argue. Never defend. Never explain something twice in the same message."));
content.push(Spacer());
content.push(Callout("The internal rhythm",
  "Acknowledge tells them you heard them. Reframe shows them the situation from a different angle. Redirect points them back to the conversation that actually solves it. If you skip the acknowledge step, the reframe sounds like a rebuttal. If you skip the reframe, the redirect sounds like a push. All three beats, every time."));
content.push(Spacer());

content.push(H2("\"$29 for a consultation? Most people do these for free.\""));
content.push(ScriptBlock("Yeah, and that is exactly the point. Most free consultations are just sales calls disguised as value. Bethany Anne charges $29 for two reasons.\n\nOne, it filters out tire-kickers. She only takes calls with owners who are serious enough to put $29 down on their own business, which means the calls she does take are higher quality, more focused, and her time is not wasted listening to pitches from people with no skin in the game.\n\nTwo, you actually get something for it. You get the Revenue Leak Audit walking you through where the money is leaking in your specific practice, plus her KPI Calculator (the same tool she uses with active clients). Even if you never work with her again, those two things are worth way more than $29.\n\nHonestly, if $29 is the thing that stops you from finding out where you are losing six figures a year, that is probably a sign worth paying attention to."));

content.push(H2("\"I already have a marketing agency.\""));
content.push(ScriptBlock("Perfect, that is actually ideal.\n\nLuxora does not replace your agency, they work with whoever you already have. Part of the Revenue Leak Audit is auditing your current ad spend to make sure you are spending the right amount, on the right offer, for your specific market. And honestly, about half the time they find things that could be running a lot better, sometimes saving owners thousands a month on spend that was not pulling its weight.\n\nIf your agency is solid, Luxora works alongside them and fixes the inside of the business so the leads they send actually convert: front desk handoff, consult conversion, package structure, follow-up, retention.\n\nIf the audit shows your spend is not being maximized, you have options. Her team also runs Meta ads in-house and has a seamless track record of strong ROI when they take over, so that is there if you want it. But no pressure to switch."));

content.push(H2("\"How much does this cost?\""));
